term IdempotencyKey {
  id: "@community/term-idempotency-key"
  version: "1.0.0"

  meaning: "A client-supplied unique identifier (typically a UUID v4 or business-event id) attached to a non-idempotent request (POST, PATCH) that allows the server to safely deduplicate retries. The server stores the (key → response) mapping for a retention window (commonly 24 hours); subsequent requests with the same key return the original response without re-executing the operation."

  domain: api-design

  _meta: {
    license: "Apache-2.0"
    license-provenance: "package-authorship-claim"
  }


  aliases: ["Idempotency-Key", "request-id (in some APIs)", "client-token (AWS)"]

  notes: [
    "Standardized as an IETF draft (`draft-ietf-httpapi-idempotency-key-header`) — the `Idempotency-Key` HTTP request header.",
    "Client generates a fresh UUID per logical operation (one charge attempt = one key). Retries of the same logical operation reuse the same key.",
    "Server stores: idempotency-key, request-fingerprint (hash of method+path+body), response-status, response-body, expiry. Lookup on incoming request: if key exists AND fingerprint matches, replay the stored response. If key exists AND fingerprint differs, return 409 Conflict.",
    "Retention window: Stripe stores 24h, AWS 5min-24h depending on service. Window MUST exceed maximum retry horizon of any client (network timeouts, browser back-button retries).",
    "Storage backend: Redis with TTL, DynamoDB with TTL, or relational table with cron cleanup. Atomic insert-if-not-exists is the critical primitive.",
    "Without an idempotency key, retries on transient failures (504 gateway timeout, network hiccup) cause duplicate writes. The 'double charge' is the canonical failure mode.",
    "Stripe pioneered the pattern in their public API (2014) and documented it as `Idempotency-Key` header; AWS uses `client-token` parameter; some APIs use a request-id / correlation-id with overlapping semantics.",
    "Idempotency-Key on GET is meaningless — GET is already idempotent per RFC 9110 (see @community/fact-http-method-semantics).",
    "Pair with @community/principle-idempotent-writes for the broader discipline. The header is the wire-format expression of the principle.",
  ]

  related: [
    @community/principle-idempotent-writes,
    @community/fact-http-method-semantics,
    @community/rule-resource-not-action,
    @community/anti-pattern-rpc-as-rest,
    @community/collection-rest-api-discipline,
  ]
}
